WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.170
This paper, G2N2, presented at ICLR 2024,

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:09.141
asks a deceptively simple question: can we design a
graph neural network whose expressive

00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:11.556
power is guaranteed by construction,

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:13.836
rather than proved after the fact?

00:00:13.916 --> 00:00:15.581
The authors give a recipe.

00:00:15.661 --> 00:00:22.758
They take a fragment of an algebraic matrix
language known to match the third-order Weisfeiler-Lehman test,

00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:28.259
write it as a context-free grammar, prune the
grammar down to its essential rules,

00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:33.357
and then translate those rules directly into the
layers of a neural network.

00:00:33.437 --> 00:00:38.992
The result is a provably three-W-L graph neural
network that is both principled and,

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:42.950
in practice, faster and more accurate than its
competitors.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.892
How expressive is a graph neural network?

00:00:45.972 --> 00:00:50.725
For years the field has answered that with the
Weisfeiler-Lehman hierarchy,

00:00:50.805 --> 00:00:55.881
and the gold standard has been to design a model
and then prove it matches, say,

00:00:55.961 --> 00:00:57.234
the third-order test.

00:00:57.314 --> 00:01:01.423
But that proof comes after the design, almost as an
afterthought.

00:01:01.503 --> 00:01:07.416
What has been missing is a systematic way to go the
other direction: to start from a language

00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:10.509
we already know is exactly as powerful as 3-W-L,

00:01:10.589 --> 00:01:14.247
and mechanically build a network that inherits that power.

00:01:14.327 --> 00:01:19.660
Without such a recipe, every expressive
architecture is a fresh, hand-crafted proof.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.848
The seed of the idea comes from a groundbreaking
observation: the 1-W-L and 3-W-L tests

00:01:25.928 --> 00:01:30.722
can each be rewritten as a fragment of a matrix
language called MATLANG.

00:01:30.802 --> 00:01:37.016
Two graphs look the same to 3-W-L if and only if
every sentence you can write in the fragment

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.724
ML-of-L-three gives them the same value.

00:01:39.804 --> 00:01:43.852
That is a beautiful bridge between combinatorics
and algebra.

00:01:43.932 --> 00:01:45.680
But a bridge is not a road.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.793
Turning one of these fragments into an actual,

00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:52.177
trainable network had been done only case by case,

00:01:52.257 --> 00:01:56.644
and the resulting models could not claim the full
3-W-L guarantee.

00:01:56.724 --> 00:02:00.570
The motivation here is to pave that road once and for all.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.640
The paper makes four contributions.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.410
First,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.180
a generic framework that turns any fragment of an
algebraic language into a graph neural

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.950
network through context-free grammars.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.720
Second, it runs that framework on the ML-of-L-three
fragment and out comes G2N2,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.490
a network that is provably 3-W-L.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.260
Third, it validates the rule set experimentally,

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.030
showing that the grammar reduction keeps
expressiveness while trimming redundancy.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.800
And fourth, across a broad battery of downstream tasks,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.570
G2N2 beats the existing 3-W-L networks, often while
running faster.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:41.210
Here is the recipe in three moves.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:47.059
Start from the operation set L-three: matrix
product, transpose, the all-ones vector,

00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:49.467
diagonal, and element-wise product.

00:02:49.547 --> 00:02:55.523
Write down an exhaustive grammar whose sentences
are exactly the fragment ML-of-L-three.

00:02:55.603 --> 00:02:57.243
Then reduce that grammar,

00:02:57.323 --> 00:03:03.368
stripping away redundant rules and variables until
only the essential productions remain,

00:03:03.448 --> 00:03:06.809
while proving each step keeps the 3-W-L guarantee.

00:03:06.889 --> 00:03:12.589
Now the magic: the surviving variables tell you
what the network's inputs should be,

00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:16.099
and each surviving rule becomes a piece of a layer.

00:03:16.179 --> 00:03:20.572
Concretely, a layer carries an edge memory C and a
node memory H;

00:03:20.652 --> 00:03:24.425
learnable linear blocks combine slices of these tensors,

00:03:24.505 --> 00:03:28.761
the reduced rules like M-times-M and M-Hadamard-M
are computed,

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:32.270
and two small MLPs stitch everything back together.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:37.500
Stack these layers, add permutation-equivariant
readouts, and you have G2N2.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.284
The evaluation spans three very different arenas.

00:03:41.364 --> 00:03:46.900
For regression, the QM9 dataset of one hundred
thirty thousand small molecules,

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.023
with twelve quantum-chemical targets, including R-squared,

00:03:51.103 --> 00:03:52.943
the hardest one to predict.

00:03:53.023 --> 00:03:56.213
For classification, the classic TUD benchmark,

00:03:56.293 --> 00:04:02.043
six datasets ranging from molecules like MUTAG and
PTC to social graphs like IMDB.

00:04:02.123 --> 00:04:04.247
And for a spectral stress test,

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:11.996
a node-regression task on nine-hundred-node graphs
that asks whether the model can act as a band-pass filter.

00:04:12.076 --> 00:04:14.911
Together they probe accuracy, generality,

00:04:14.991 --> 00:04:19.390
and a subtle spectral ability that trips up other
3-W-L models.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:24.038
The headline result is that G2N2 does not just
match the theory,

00:04:24.118 --> 00:04:25.737
it dominates in practice.

00:04:25.817 --> 00:04:28.387
On QM9, learning targets one at a time,

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:33.755
it posts the best error on every single target
while training faster than PPGN.

00:04:33.835 --> 00:04:36.540
On the notoriously hard R-squared target,

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.598
its error drops to zero-point-three-four-two,

00:04:39.678 --> 00:04:44.830
where PPGN sits at three-point-seven-eight, more
than a ten-fold improvement,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.654
and when all twelve targets are learned at once the
gap widens further.

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:55.701
On graph classification it beats the second-best
network on five of the six TUD datasets.

00:04:55.781 --> 00:05:00.593
And on the spectral test it cleanly learns
band-pass filters where PPGN,

00:05:00.673 --> 00:05:04.330
starved of the memory it would need, essentially fails.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.852
The most instructive experiment is the
grammar-reduction ablation.

00:05:08.932 --> 00:05:12.562
The authors compare the full grammar, an intermediate one,

00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:16.848
and the reduced grammar r-G-of-L-three on the QM9
R-squared target.

00:05:16.928 --> 00:05:19.279
Their errors are essentially the same,

00:05:19.359 --> 00:05:24.844
which confirms that reduction throws away
redundancy without touching expressive power.

00:05:24.924 --> 00:05:29.833
But when you push past the reduced grammar and
start deleting essential rules,

00:05:29.913 --> 00:05:32.456
performance degrades in a measurable way.

00:05:32.536 --> 00:05:38.725
That degradation is actually useful information: it
tells you how much each operation contributes,

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:44.610
so you can prune the model deliberately when a task
does not demand the full 3-W-L strength.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.160
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.932
On QM9's R-squared target,

00:05:49.012 --> 00:05:53.704
error falls from three-point-seven-eight to
zero-point-three-four-two,

00:05:53.784 --> 00:05:55.953
roughly an eleven-fold reduction,

00:05:56.033 --> 00:06:02.361
and the model does it in ninety-eight seconds per
epoch versus PPGN's one hundred twenty-nine.

00:06:02.441 --> 00:06:04.474
On the spectral band-pass task,

00:06:04.554 --> 00:06:10.132
its R-squared score of zero-point-eight-two towers
over PPGN's zero-point-one-zero.

00:06:10.212 --> 00:06:16.540
And on the TUD classification suite it lands better
than second place on five of six datasets,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.880
including ninety-two-and-a-half percent on MUTAG.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.994
The lasting message is a change of workflow.

00:06:23.074 --> 00:06:28.047
Instead of designing a graph network and then
hoping to prove it is expressive,

00:06:28.127 --> 00:06:32.524
you can start from a language whose expressive
power you already know,

00:06:32.604 --> 00:06:37.256
reduce it to a clean grammar, and read the network
straight off the rules,

00:06:37.336 --> 00:06:39.942
expressiveness guaranteed by construction.

00:06:40.022 --> 00:06:45.571
G2N2 is the concrete payoff of that idea: a
provably 3-W-L model that is faster and more

00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:47.553
accurate than its predecessors.

00:06:47.633 --> 00:06:49.920
And because the framework is generic,

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.292
the same grammatical route could turn other
algebraic fragments into other networks,

00:06:55.372 --> 00:06:58.170
each carrying its expressive power by design.
